WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
This lesson walks through the Cloud Agent SDK, formerly called the Cloud Code SDK,

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.000
the programmatic interface for controlling sessions from your own code. We'll look at

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.840
what it can provide, how to create and manage sessions, how to stream output and tool events,

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.079
and when to pick it over a direct API call. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.959
What changes when the thing driving a Cloud Code session isn't a person at a keyboard? That's what

6
00:00:28.959 --> 00:00:35.599
the Cloud Agent SDK is for. It used to be called the Cloud Code SDK, and it's the programmatic

7
00:00:35.599 --> 00:00:41.200
interface that lets you integrate Cloud Code into custom applications, scripts, and pipelines.

8
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.360
Instead of typing commands into a terminal, you call SDK methods from your code to start sessions,

9
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.360
inject prompts, and handle output. A programmatic session is one you create and manage that way

10
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:59.680
rather than through the interactive CLI. Your application decides when sessions start and stop

11
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.360
and how their output is processed. Session streaming is the capability to receive tool

12
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.040
call events and output incrementally as the session runs, giving you visibility into every

13
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.680
step without waiting for a complete response. The SDK and the command line interface expose

14
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:24.480
the same underlying Cloud Code capabilities. The difference is who or what is driving the session.

15
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.720
With the CLI, a developer types commands into a terminal and reads results interactively. That

16
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:37.120
works well for one-off tasks and exploratory work. With the SDK, your code calls methods,

17
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:43.360
passes prompts programmatically, and processes results however your application needs. There's

18
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.760
no terminal involved at all. Use the CLI when a human is in the loop and driving the work.

19
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.879
Switch to the SDK when your code needs to be in the driver's seat, whether that's a script, a

20
00:01:54.879 --> 00:02:01.120
pipeline step, or a long-running background service. This is the decision that trips up a lot of

21
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:07.919
developers. When do you use the SDK versus calling the Cloud API directly? The SDK is for agentic

22
00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:14.080
sessions. It manages the full agent loop, meaning multi-step tool use, file edits, shell commands,

23
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:20.399
and streaming output. You give it a prompt and it handles everything in between. The direct API is

24
00:02:20.399 --> 00:02:26.320
for model calls. Send a prompt, get a completion. There's no session state and no built-in tool

25
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:32.320
orchestration unless you build it yourself. If you need the agent loop or you need real-time visibility

26
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.000
into what the agent is doing, use the SDK. If you need a single model call or you're building

27
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.320
your own agent framework and want to own the orchestration, use the API. Creating a session

28
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.279
programmatically starts with instantiating a session object in your code. You configure it

29
00:02:49.279 --> 00:02:54.960
with the model you want to use, the tool categories you're allowing, the working directory, and any

30
00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.839
environment variables the session needs to access. Once configured, you pass prompts as plain strings.

31
00:03:01.919 --> 00:03:07.919
There's no terminal, no interactive input, no manual step. The SDK handles the session loop

32
00:03:07.919 --> 00:03:13.279
from there. This approach makes it straightforward to embed cloud code in Python scripts,

33
00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:20.720
Node.js services, CI runners, or any other runtime environment that can call the SDK libraries.

34
00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.559
The session behaves the same way regardless of how it's invoked. Once a session is running, you can

35
00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:32.000
shape its behavior by injecting context and instructions at different points. At creation time,

36
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.160
you pass a system prompt to establish role, tone, and behavioral constraints before any user turn

37
00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.759
begins. You can also inject file contents or directory listings, so the session starts with

38
00:03:43.759 --> 00:03:50.559
relevant code-based knowledge already loaded. Tool permissions are set at creation too, letting you

39
00:03:50.559 --> 00:03:56.960
explicitly allow or restrict read, write, and shell access to enforce safety boundaries.

40
00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:02.639
And you can append follow-up prompts mid-session to redirect the agent without restarting,

41
00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:09.279
so it keeps the context it's already accumulated. Streaming is one of the most useful SDK features

42
00:04:09.279 --> 00:04:14.639
for production applications. Instead of blocking until the session completes, your code receives

43
00:04:14.639 --> 00:04:20.959
events as they happen in real time. Tool call events fire each time the agent reads a file,

44
00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:26.640
writes output, or runs a shell command. That lets you log exactly what the agent is doing

45
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.440
without waiting for the session to finish. Output chunks arrive incrementally,

46
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.359
so you can display live progress or pipe results downstream. Error events surface failures

47
00:04:37.359 --> 00:04:43.760
immediately, giving your code a chance to react, retry, or alert an operator. For long-running

48
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.679
sessions, streaming isn't a nice-to-have. It's the difference between a responsive application

49
00:04:49.679 --> 00:04:56.640
and one that hangs silently waiting for a final result. The SDK's primary use case is embedding

50
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:03.119
clawed code capabilities into automated systems your team already owns. Code review bots can

51
00:05:03.119 --> 00:05:09.519
trigger SDK sessions when a pull request opens, stream the analysis, and post results directly

52
00:05:09.519 --> 00:05:14.880
into GitHub comments without any human intervention. Documentation generators

53
00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:22.640
can pass source files into a session and receive updated docs in return. CI and CD pipelines can

54
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:29.279
launch clawed code sessions as explicit pipeline steps, with structured exit codes that let the

55
00:05:29.279 --> 00:05:35.119
rest of the pipeline react to what happened. Any workflow that benefits from agentic code

56
00:05:35.119 --> 00:05:41.200
understanding but needs to run inside a larger automated system is a strong candidate for SDK

57
00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:48.799
integration. The Clawed Agent SDK gives you full programmatic control over agentic sessions,

58
00:05:48.799 --> 00:05:54.160
from creation and context injection through streaming output and tool event handling.

59
00:05:54.799 --> 00:06:00.480
It runs the whole agent loop for you, which is the line that separates it from a direct API call.

60
00:06:01.119 --> 00:06:06.640
Reach for it when your code, not a human, needs to drive a multi-step workflow,

61
00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.799
and reach for the API when a single completion is all the job requires.

62
00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.480
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

